1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:04,408
There is a star you can see tonight that hides a secret.

2
00:00:04,408 --> 00:00:10,376
Right beside it sits a second star, ten thousand times fainter, lost in the glare.

3
00:00:10,376 --> 00:00:12,122
No eye has ever seen it.

4
00:00:12,122 --> 00:00:14,233
Not until eighteen sixty-two.

5
00:00:14,699 --> 00:00:23,408
And yet, a book published in nineteen seventy-six said that a people in Mali, in West Africa, had known about it for centuries.

6
00:00:23,408 --> 00:00:24,779
Without a telescope.

7
00:00:24,779 --> 00:00:26,699
Without a single instrument.

8
00:00:27,166 --> 00:00:33,809
The explanation on offer: amphibious beings from that very star, who came down to teach them.

9
00:00:33,809 --> 00:00:40,666
The ancient astronaut world calls it the Sirius Mystery, and it is one of its most famous cases.

10
00:00:41,133 --> 00:00:43,721
Tonight we test it in a different way.

11
00:00:43,721 --> 00:00:49,375
We follow the claim backwards, the way you would follow a photocopy of a photocopy.

12
00:00:49,375 --> 00:00:51,486
Each generation loses a little.

13
00:00:51,486 --> 00:00:53,666
And each one adds something new.

14
00:00:54,133 --> 00:00:56,025
Four generations, one star.

15
00:00:56,025 --> 00:01:01,563
By the end, a verdict: is it a message from the sky, or an echo from the Earth?

16
00:01:01,563 --> 00:01:03,666
Let's start with the original.

17
00:01:08,133 --> 00:01:09,267
Generation zero.

18
00:01:09,267 --> 00:01:10,188
The original.

19
00:01:10,188 --> 00:01:16,355
Sirius is the brightest star in our night sky, about eight and a half light years away.

20
00:01:16,355 --> 00:01:20,466
Every culture that could see it has told stories about it.

21
00:01:20,933 --> 00:01:28,495
In eighteen forty-four, the German astronomer Friedrich Bessel noticed Sirius was wobbling across the sky.

22
00:01:28,495 --> 00:01:30,991
Something unseen was pulling on it.

23
00:01:30,991 --> 00:01:35,700
A hidden companion, he reasoned, orbiting every fifty years or so.

24
00:01:36,166 --> 00:01:48,233
Eighteen years later, on the last day of January eighteen sixty-two, the American telescope maker Alvan Graham Clark was testing a brand new eighteen and a half inch lens.

25
00:01:48,233 --> 00:01:49,433
And there it was.

26
00:01:49,900 --> 00:01:52,920
A tiny white spark right beside the glare.

27
00:01:52,920 --> 00:01:53,567
Sirius B.

28
00:01:53,567 --> 00:02:02,699
It is faint, about ten thousand times dimmer than Sirius A, and it can only be seen through a good telescope, on a clear night.

29
00:02:03,166 --> 00:02:05,662
What it is was only worked out later.

30
00:02:05,662 --> 00:02:11,735
In nineteen fifteen its spectrum showed it was hot but dim, which meant it had to be tiny.

31
00:02:11,735 --> 00:02:17,199
A white dwarf: about the mass of our Sun, squeezed into a ball the size of Earth.

32
00:02:17,666 --> 00:02:20,428
A teaspoon of it would weigh many tons.

33
00:02:20,428 --> 00:02:23,968
By the nineteen twenties, astronomers accepted it.

34
00:02:23,968 --> 00:02:26,376
The orbit takes about fifty years.

35
00:02:26,376 --> 00:02:29,633
That is the target the Dogon claim has to hit.

36
00:02:30,099 --> 00:02:31,427
Remember that date.

37
00:02:31,427 --> 00:02:37,224
By the nineteen twenties, Sirius B was in textbooks, in magazines, and in lectures.

38
00:02:37,224 --> 00:02:43,999
The knowledge was public, in Europe, a quarter of a century before the Dogon story reached print.

39
00:02:44,466 --> 00:02:46,241
So here is the original.

40
00:02:46,241 --> 00:02:49,199
A real, invisible, fifty-year companion.

41
00:02:49,199 --> 00:02:53,933
Now the first copy, and the strange thing about how it was made.

42
00:02:54,400 --> 00:02:55,469
Generation one.

43
00:02:55,469 --> 00:03:01,029
A French anthropologist, Marcel Griaule, and his colleague Germaine Dieterlen.

44
00:03:01,029 --> 00:03:08,300
Griaule first went to Dogon country in nineteen thirty-one, and kept going back for twenty-five years.

45
00:03:08,766 --> 00:03:18,875
The Dogon live along the Bandiagara Escarpment, a five hundred metre sandstone cliff, about three hundred kilometres south of Timbuktu.

46
00:03:18,875 --> 00:03:24,566
Their villages cling to the rock, a thousand years of history and tradition.

47
00:03:25,033 --> 00:03:40,266
In late nineteen forty-six, Griaule spent thirty-three days in conversation with a blind elder named Ogotemmêli, who taught him the main symbols of Dogon religion, as he had learned them from his father and grandfather.

48
00:03:40,733 --> 00:03:43,190
The Sirius material comes next.

49
00:03:43,190 --> 00:03:50,405
In nineteen fifty, Griaule and Dieterlen published a paper called A Sudanese Sirius System.

50
00:03:50,405 --> 00:03:55,400
It describes a star the Dogon call Pō tolo, the Digitaria star.

51
00:03:55,866 --> 00:04:00,250
Digitaria is a grain, a tiny one, the smallest the Dogon know.

52
00:04:00,250 --> 00:04:07,533
And that, the paper says, is what the star is: small, and, in their words, the heaviest thing there is.

53
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:13,000
They said it circles Sirius in an ellipse, with Sirius at one focus.

54
00:04:13,000 --> 00:04:14,544
It spins on its axis.

55
00:04:14,544 --> 00:04:16,750
And one lap takes fifty years.

56
00:04:16,750 --> 00:04:20,133
To an astronomer, that sounds like a bullseye.

57
00:04:20,600 --> 00:04:26,041
There is a ceremony tied to it, the Sigui, held about every sixty years.

58
00:04:26,041 --> 00:04:28,989
The last began in nineteen sixty-seven.

59
00:04:28,989 --> 00:04:33,600
A second companion, Ęmmę Ya, the sorghum star, was named too.

60
00:04:34,066 --> 00:04:37,410
Notice what Griaule and Dieterlen did not claim.

61
00:04:37,410 --> 00:04:39,081
They did not say aliens.

62
00:04:39,081 --> 00:04:41,310
They did not say it was ancient.

63
00:04:41,310 --> 00:04:48,833
Their own words: how, without instruments, men could know this, quote, has not been settled, nor even posed.

64
00:04:49,300 --> 00:04:55,061
But a scholar's careful puzzle was about to be photocopied into something much bigger.

65
00:04:55,061 --> 00:04:56,066
Generation two.

66
00:04:56,533 --> 00:04:57,610
Generation two.

67
00:04:57,610 --> 00:04:59,117
Nineteen seventy-six.

68
00:04:59,117 --> 00:05:04,430
Robert Temple, an American writer in London, publishes The Sirius Mystery.

69
00:05:04,430 --> 00:05:10,533
He reads Griaule's papers and asks the one question the anthropologists left hanging.

70
00:05:11,000 --> 00:05:14,178
His answer, in four hundred pages, is aliens.

71
00:05:14,178 --> 00:05:24,633
Beings called the Nommo, amphibious, from the Sirius system, who landed in an ark some five thousand years ago and taught civilisation to the Earth.

72
00:05:25,100 --> 00:05:33,402
In Dogon tradition, the Nommo are ancestral spirits, water beings, green, with serpent-like bodies below the waist.

73
00:05:33,402 --> 00:05:38,600
Temple read them as extraterrestrials, and a descending ark as a rocket.

74
00:05:39,066 --> 00:05:43,504
He tied the Dogon to ancient Egypt, to Sumer, to Greek myths.

75
00:05:43,504 --> 00:05:53,399
By the second edition, in nineteen ninety-eight, the subtitle promised new scientific evidence of alien contact five thousand years ago.

76
00:05:53,866 --> 00:05:56,805
The book was reviewed in Time magazine.

77
00:05:56,805 --> 00:06:02,757
And it wore famous names: Temple said Isaac Asimov had found no mistakes in it.

78
00:06:02,757 --> 00:06:06,600
Asimov later said he had found it too hard to read.

79
00:06:07,066 --> 00:06:08,799
That was the second copy.

80
00:06:08,799 --> 00:06:16,699
And with it, a people in Mali became famous around the world for a star story that later fieldwork could not find.

81
00:06:17,166 --> 00:06:18,933
Now the third generation.

82
00:06:18,933 --> 00:06:21,266
The one most of us actually meet.

83
00:06:21,733 --> 00:06:23,041
Generation three.

84
00:06:23,041 --> 00:06:24,042
The internet.

85
00:06:24,042 --> 00:06:29,352
Search for the Dogon and you will find the claim stated as flat fact.

86
00:06:29,352 --> 00:06:32,892
Sirius B, fifty-year orbit, ancient knowledge.

87
00:06:32,892 --> 00:06:34,585
The caveats fall away.

88
00:06:34,585 --> 00:06:37,433
Just like a photocopy of a photocopy.

89
00:06:37,899 --> 00:06:46,078
Websites and documentaries added their own touches: dates thousands of years old, Egyptian roots, lost knowledge.

90
00:06:46,078 --> 00:06:49,045
Those come from Temple and later writers.

91
00:06:49,045 --> 00:06:53,966
Griaule himself made no claim about great antiquity, or about Egypt.

92
00:06:54,433 --> 00:07:00,543
Others said the Dogon could see Sirius B with the naked eye, thanks to special eyesight.

93
00:07:00,543 --> 00:07:04,917
There is no evidence for that, and it is physically impossible.

94
00:07:04,917 --> 00:07:08,666
Sirius B is far too faint, and too close to the glare.

95
00:07:09,133 --> 00:07:11,822
It is worth pausing on how this happens.

96
00:07:11,822 --> 00:07:15,722
Each retelling is clean, confident, and stripped of doubt.

97
00:07:15,722 --> 00:07:20,833
The original authors were more careful than almost everyone who quoted them.

98
00:07:21,300 --> 00:07:25,996
So let us rebuild the claim from the beginning and see how much is left.

99
00:07:25,996 --> 00:07:27,692
Which parts are checkable?

100
00:07:27,692 --> 00:07:28,670
Which are hits?

101
00:07:28,670 --> 00:07:31,866
And which are just imagination working backwards?

102
00:07:32,333 --> 00:07:35,677
The Dogon claim, strictly, has six parts.

103
00:07:35,677 --> 00:07:37,227
A companion exists.

104
00:07:37,227 --> 00:07:38,124
It is tiny.

105
00:07:38,124 --> 00:07:39,103
It is heavy.

106
00:07:39,103 --> 00:07:40,490
It orbits Sirius.

107
00:07:40,490 --> 00:07:42,529
The orbit is fifty years.

108
00:07:42,529 --> 00:07:45,302
And Sirius has a second companion.

109
00:07:45,302 --> 00:07:46,199
One by one.

110
00:07:46,666 --> 00:07:49,269
On paper, the first five are right.

111
00:07:49,269 --> 00:07:51,648
That is why this story has legs.

112
00:07:51,648 --> 00:07:57,597
A guess about a tiny, heavy, fifty-year companion would be a lucky guess indeed.

113
00:07:57,597 --> 00:07:58,935
If it was a guess.

114
00:07:58,935 --> 00:08:00,125
If it was Dogon.

115
00:08:00,125 --> 00:08:01,166
If it was old.

116
00:08:01,633 --> 00:08:03,154
Those are three big ifs.

117
00:08:03,154 --> 00:08:07,399
Time to go back to the field, and see whether anyone else heard it.

118
00:08:07,866 --> 00:08:11,213
The first big test came in nineteen ninety-one.

119
00:08:11,213 --> 00:08:21,966
The Dutch anthropologist Walter van Beek, who had worked in Dogon country for years, published a paper called Dogon Restudied, in Current Anthropology.

120
00:08:22,433 --> 00:08:24,234
His question was simple.

121
00:08:24,234 --> 00:08:29,037
Do the Dogon of today recognise the cosmology Griaule described?

122
00:08:29,037 --> 00:08:35,566
He did fieldwork in the villages of Tireli and Sanga, where Griaule himself had worked.

123
00:08:36,033 --> 00:08:40,320
He found that people did talk about a star called Sigu tolo.

124
00:08:40,320 --> 00:08:44,178
But they disagreed completely about which star it was.

125
00:08:44,178 --> 00:08:49,180
For some it was an invisible star that rises to announce the festival.

126
00:08:49,180 --> 00:08:50,966
For another it was Venus.

127
00:08:51,433 --> 00:08:56,701
None of his informants described Sirius as a double or triple system.

128
00:08:56,701 --> 00:09:00,214
None gave a fifty-year orbit, or a heavy star.

129
00:09:00,214 --> 00:09:04,566
Sirius, to some, was simply dana tolo, the hunter's star.

130
00:09:05,033 --> 00:09:11,123
He also found that the Sigui is scheduled by counting years, not by watching stars.

131
00:09:11,123 --> 00:09:16,333
The star connection was not how the Dogon themselves kept the calendar.

132
00:09:16,799 --> 00:09:19,816
And then the line that made the field famous.

133
00:09:19,816 --> 00:09:24,709
Quote: all agree, however, that they learned about the star from Griaule.

134
00:09:24,709 --> 00:09:25,380
End quote.

135
00:09:25,380 --> 00:09:28,999
On that account, the star story came from the visitor.

136
00:09:29,466 --> 00:09:31,187
The reaction was fierce.

137
00:09:31,187 --> 00:09:41,942
Griaule's daughter, the anthropologist Geneviève Calame-Griaule, replied that van Beek had not gone through the proper steps to be told Dogon secrets.

138
00:09:41,942 --> 00:09:44,666
Others called his dismissal political.

139
00:09:45,133 --> 00:09:48,595
So we have two honest fieldworkers who disagree.

140
00:09:48,595 --> 00:09:50,760
One heard a deep secret, once.

141
00:09:50,760 --> 00:09:53,212
The other could not find it again.

142
00:09:53,212 --> 00:09:55,593
Both cannot be a clean photocopy.

143
00:09:55,593 --> 00:09:59,200
So how could a modern fact end up in an old story?

144
00:09:59,666 --> 00:10:00,885
Route one: people.

145
00:10:00,885 --> 00:10:06,437
The Dogon were among the last in West Africa to lose their independence to France.

146
00:10:06,437 --> 00:10:11,785
Colonial officers, teachers, missionaries and traders moved through the region.

147
00:10:11,785 --> 00:10:14,900
Any of them could have talked about the stars.

148
00:10:15,366 --> 00:10:17,143
Route two: an expedition.

149
00:10:17,143 --> 00:10:27,805
Astronomer Noah Brosch points to the French eclipse expedition of eighteen ninety-three, led by Henri Deslandres, which spent five weeks in the field.

150
00:10:27,805 --> 00:10:30,933
It is reasonable to think there was contact.

151
00:10:31,399 --> 00:10:33,067
Route three: travellers.

152
00:10:33,067 --> 00:10:37,724
James Oberg pointed out that the Dogon were not an isolated people.

153
00:10:37,724 --> 00:10:42,866
A single member could have picked up the idea abroad, and carried it home.

154
00:10:43,333 --> 00:10:46,582
Route four, and the quietest: the science press.

155
00:10:46,582 --> 00:10:50,034
In the nineteen twenties, Sirius B was in the news.

156
00:10:50,034 --> 00:10:54,163
Several observers even claimed to see a third star beside it.

157
00:10:54,163 --> 00:11:01,000
Oberg noted that the Dogon version looks like what Europe was guessing in the late nineteen twenties.

158
00:11:01,466 --> 00:11:03,743
Route five: the interview itself.

159
00:11:03,743 --> 00:11:15,266
Critics note that the Sirius account rested on a few elders, a translator, and an investigator who wanted African thought to be taken as seriously as Greek philosophy.

160
00:11:15,733 --> 00:11:17,515
Imagine the conversation.

161
00:11:17,515 --> 00:11:20,723
A friendly visitor shows interest in the sky.

162
00:11:20,723 --> 00:11:24,287
He asks if there is a small star near the big one.

163
00:11:24,287 --> 00:11:26,640
An elder, being polite, says yes.

164
00:11:26,640 --> 00:11:30,133
And later, in print, that becomes ancient wisdom.

165
00:11:30,600 --> 00:11:37,139
Anthropologists call it contamination: when the researcher's questions shape the answers.

166
00:11:37,139 --> 00:11:39,270
It is no accusation of fraud.

167
00:11:39,270 --> 00:11:44,266
Van Beek proposed a mix of cultures, a bricolage, rather than a lie.

168
00:11:44,733 --> 00:11:46,047
Back to the stars.

169
00:11:46,047 --> 00:11:47,944
Temple set himself a test.

170
00:11:47,944 --> 00:11:57,433
If a Sirius C is ever found, and it is a red dwarf, he wrote, I will conclude that the Dogon information has been fully validated.

171
00:11:57,899 --> 00:12:04,323
In nineteen ninety-five, two French astronomers studied tiny wobbles in the Sirius pair.

172
00:12:04,323 --> 00:12:08,776
A third body orbiting every six years could not be ruled out.

173
00:12:08,776 --> 00:12:10,966
A Sirius C was still possible.

174
00:12:11,433 --> 00:12:16,681
Then in two thousand eight, high-contrast infrared imaging looked closely.

175
00:12:16,681 --> 00:12:25,899
The result: the chance of a third star in the Sirius system is now low, though the space near Sirius A could not be fully checked.

176
00:12:26,366 --> 00:12:30,508
That study pointed to a faint brown dwarf, not a red dwarf.

177
00:12:30,508 --> 00:12:40,266
And astronomer Jay Holberg notes such a body would be far too faint, and too close to Sirius A, to explain the nineteen twenties sightings.

178
00:12:40,733 --> 00:12:48,157
As for the aliens: Ian Ridpath, in nineteen seventy-eight, showed the Sirius system is a hostile address.

179
00:12:48,157 --> 00:12:53,602
Sirius B once swelled into a red giant, which would have wrecked any planets.

180
00:12:53,602 --> 00:12:57,066
Stable orbits in the habitable zone do not exist.

181
00:12:57,533 --> 00:13:01,627
So the test came back, and not in the Dogon claim's favour.

182
00:13:01,627 --> 00:13:03,085
Time for the debrief.

183
00:13:03,085 --> 00:13:06,000
Evidence on one side, theory on the other.

184
00:13:06,466 --> 00:13:07,360
The scorecard.

185
00:13:07,360 --> 00:13:11,700
We rate every part of the claim, from the star itself to the aliens.

186
00:13:12,166 --> 00:13:18,921
A white dwarf companion to Sirius, orbiting every fifty years: evidence, fully confirmed.

187
00:13:18,921 --> 00:13:26,436
The Dogon told Griaule about a companion star: partly, a claim reported by one team of researchers.

188
00:13:26,436 --> 00:13:29,700
Older than eighteen sixty-two: no evidence.

189
00:13:30,166 --> 00:13:33,103
Confirmed by later fieldwork: debunked.

190
00:13:33,103 --> 00:13:38,600
European contact as the source: likely, never proven, still the best fit.

191
00:13:38,600 --> 00:13:41,838
Amphibious aliens from Sirius: no evidence.

192
00:13:41,838 --> 00:13:46,733
A second companion, Sirius C: unverified, and probably not there.

193
00:13:47,200 --> 00:13:53,007
Three generations of copies, and each one added certainty the original never had.

194
00:13:53,007 --> 00:13:56,233
That is what a photocopy of a photocopy does.

195
00:13:56,700 --> 00:13:57,559
The verdict.

196
00:13:57,559 --> 00:13:58,490
Contaminated.

197
00:13:58,490 --> 00:13:59,708
The star is real.

198
00:13:59,708 --> 00:14:09,233
The mystery is not that the Dogon knew it, but that a story about it was recorded, retold, and gradually turned into proof of aliens.

199
00:14:09,700 --> 00:14:15,181
And the Dogon deserve better than a footnote in someone else's alien story.

200
00:14:15,181 --> 00:14:21,466
They are a real people with a rich cosmology, masks, music, and a sixty-year festival.

201
00:14:21,466 --> 00:14:24,170
The sky was always important to them.

202
00:14:24,170 --> 00:14:25,266
That is enough.

203
00:14:25,733 --> 00:14:28,660
Next case: March nineteen ninety-seven.

204
00:14:28,660 --> 00:14:35,865
Thousands of people across Arizona see an enormous V of lights glide silently over their cities.

205
00:14:35,865 --> 00:14:36,766
What was it?

206
00:14:37,233 --> 00:14:40,979
Which generation of the story do you think lost the most?

207
00:14:40,979 --> 00:14:46,500
Tell us in the comments, and subscribe for the next ancient mystery put to the test.
